<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gibeah</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gibeah of Saul</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gibeah was a town of the tribe of Benjamin, a few kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, described in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> as the home and royal seat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saul">Saul</a>, the first king of Israel. It is usually identified with Tell el-Ful, where excavations found a fortified structure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, though its dating and connection with Saul are debated.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gibeah.jpg" alt="Gibeah" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hagai Agmon-Snir حچاي اچمون-سنير חגי אגמון-שניר · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RoyalPalaceHusseinTellElFulDec032022_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
